## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to apply fundamental security controls to third-party components—a common real-world risk at many companies. It evaluates your understanding of vendor vetting, data minimization, privacy controls, and incident response readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Third-party risk management
- Data minimization and PII redaction
- User consent and privacy controls
- Observability and incident response readiness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: redact PII from analytics payload
function redact(payload) {
  const redacted = { ...payload };
  if (redacted.userEmail) redacted.userEmail = "[REDACTED]";
  if (redacted.userPhone) redacted.userPhone = "[REDACTED]";
  return redacted;
}

// Example: hash identifiers before transmission
function hashIdentifier(identifier) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(identifier).digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to secure extension points with practical controls, not theory.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin isolation boundaries
- Manifest signing and attestation
- Runtime sandboxing and host API policy
- CI gates and security tooling
- Incident response with containment and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example manifest verification
function verifyManifest(manifest, signature, pubKey){
  if(!verifySignature(signature, manifest, pubKey)) return false;
  if(!isAllowedScope(manifest.scope)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test sandbox isolation in CI/CD and production?
- What telemetry and alarm thresholds would indicate plugin abuse?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-tenant data isolation, token binding, and incident response in a realistic multi-tenant platform similar to large-scale chat/workspace products.

## Key Concepts

- Least privilege per-automation scopes
- Token binding to tenant identity and audience (aud/iss)
- Short-lived tokens with rapid rotation
- Per-resource RBAC/ACL enforcement
- API gateway policy checks and runtime validation
- Tamper-evident audit logs and a 24h IR playbook

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation: verify JWT claims for a request
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function authorize(token, requiredScope, tenantId) {
  const payload = jwt.verify(token, PUBLIC_KEY, { algorithms:['RS256'] });
  if (payload.aud !== 'automation') return false;
  if (payload.tenant !== tenantId) return false;
  if (!payload.scopes.includes(requiredScope)) return false;
  // ensure token is recent
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you design token rotation and revocation in case of breach?
- What telemetry would you collect to detect automation token abuse?
